 ### [PNG-based Gaussian Compression](4834-png-based-gaussian-compression.md)
-A compression technique for trained 3D Gaussian splats that quantizes parameters to 8-bit or 16-bit and stores them as lossless PNG images, optionally using spatial sorting (Morton code) to improve compression ratios. It serves as a port of the PyTorch `gsplat` compression module to MLX. It achieves approximately 10x compression on standard 3DGS models. Unlike the PyTorch upstream which uses `torchpq` for k-means clustering of higher-order spherical harmonics, this method falls back to NPZ for higher-order bands due to library availability constraints. See [PNG-based Gaussian Compression](4834-png-based-gaussian-compression.md).
-
-### Gaussian Splat Texture Packing
-A variant of Gaussian Data Packing that maps the packed `uint32_t` data into a 2D OpenGL texture for GPU access [S1, unofficial]. It maps each Gaussian to two horizontal columns in a texture of width 2048 [S1, unofficial]. The x-coordinate is `(index & 0x3ff) << 1` and the y-coordinate is `index >> 10` [S1, unofficial]. The texture format is `GL_RGBA32UI` with `GL_RGBA_INTEGER` type and `GL_UNSIGNED_INT` data type [S1, unofficial]. Filters are `GL_NEAREST` for min/mag, and wrap mode is `GL_CLAMP_TO_EDGE` for both S and T axes [S1, unofficial].
+A compression technique for trained 3D Gaussian splats that quantizes parameters to 8-bit or 16-bit and stores them as lossless PNG images, optionally using spatial sorting (Morton code) to improve compression ratios. It serves as a port of the PyTorch `gsplat` compression module to MLX. It achieves approximately 10x compression on standard 3DGS models. Unlike the PyTorch upstream which uses `torchpq` for k-means clustering of higher-order spherical harmonics, this method falls back to NPZ for higher-order bands due to library availability constraints.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(n)$ to pack $n$ Gaussians, as each Gaussian is processed in constant time [G].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(n)$ for `packedData` ($8 \times 4 = 32$ bytes per Gaussian) and $O(n)$ for `worldPositions` ($3 \times 4 = 12$ bytes per Gaussian) [G]. Total 44 bytes per Gaussian [G].
-- **Performance:** The packing is CPU-bound and typically fast for moderate $n$ [G]. The resulting buffer is optimized for GPU texture uploads and direct memory access [G].
-- **Strengths:** Compact representation, efficient GPU memory usage, fast packing/unpacking [G].
-- **Limitations:** Loss of precision in covariance storage (16-bit) [G]. Position data is stored twice (bit-cast and raw float), increasing memory usage [G].
-- **When to use:** When rendering 3D Gaussian Splats on GPU with OpenGL/Vulkan, especially when sorting and rasterization require fast access to covariance and position data [G].
-- **When not to use:** When high-precision covariance is required, or when memory bandwidth is not a bottleneck and simpler data structures are preferred [G].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of Gaussians. Each Gaussian is processed in constant time.
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for `packedData` ($8N$ uint32_t) and `worldPositions` ($3N$ float).
 - **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
+- **Scaling:** Linear scaling with the number of Gaussians.
+- **Strengths:** Compact representation, efficient GPU access, simple structure.
+- **Limitations:** Loss of precision in covariance storage, fixed color range.
+- **When to use:** When efficient GPU-side access to Gaussian parameters is required.
+- **When not to use:** When high precision in covariance is critical or when compression is needed.
 
 ## Background
-Gaussian Data Packing is part of the 3D Gaussian Splatting rendering pipeline, specifically designed for efficient GPU-based rendering [S1, unofficial]. It is implemented in the `gsplat` library, an unofficial OpenGL implementation of 3D Gaussian Splatting [S1, unofficial]. The method leverages GLSL packing functions (`packHalf2x16`) and bit-casting to optimize data transfer and storage [S1, unofficial].
+Origin: The method is described in the source [S1, unofficial], which provides an implementation for packing Gaussian data for OpenGL-based rendering.
 
